            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "In other words, you can come up with cases where [this line](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.bufremove/blob/14805473144fffbd019d07e51bed2d176e065ee9/lua/mini/bufremove.lua#LL156C7-L156C7) will simply fail:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n    -- Try using previous buffer\r\n    vim.cmd('bprevious') -- <------------- this line can (and will) crash sometimes\r\n    if cur_buf ~= vim.api.nvim_win_get_buf(win_id) then return end\r\n```\r\n\r\nI can confirm that by calling `:lua vim.cmd('bprevious')` and have it fail. In this case, `vim.fn.getcmdwintype() == ''` and, additionally, the alternate buffer is NOT listed so bufremove will not try to switch back to it (maybe it should?). Given that, bufremove wrongfully assumes it can call `:bprevious` which it does and which then crashes.\r\n\r\nTwo things that can be done here:\r\n- what's the logic behind not switching to unlisted `#`-buffer? (maybe it should switch to it?)\r\n- perhaps `:bprevious` should be wrapped in a `pcall`?\r\n- if bprev pcall fails and the alternate buffer exists but is unlisted, maybe switch to it?",
